//? Title: Zobrist Hashing: Updating a Digit One Pixel at a Time
//? Subtitle: XOR cancellation, collision expectations and the limits of random-looking hashes
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A Zobrist hash assigns a random key to each cell/category pair and XORs the
keys belonging to a configuration. Replacing one cell takes two key lookups
and two XORs, regardless of the configuration's size. We apply this idea to
the 8 by 8 handwritten images in `dm::datasets::digits()`: grey levels are
categories, and changing pixels is the analogue of changing board squares.
We derive the update identity and the expected number of colliding pairs,
verify every step of an image transformation, and compare truncated hashes
across 64 seeded key tables. A four-configuration identity explains why
these hashes must not be treated as fully independent random numbers.
)

\toc

> The data: small images as categorical configurations

The built-in digits frame contains 1797 images, each with 64 integer pixel
values from 0 (white) to 16 (dark), plus a numeric digit label. The image,
rather than its class, is the object to identify. Remove `digit` before
encoding: otherwise two identical images with different labels would hash
differently. The remaining column order is row-major pixel order.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  std::print("{}\n", digits.select({"pixel_0", "pixel_1", "pixel_2", "pixel_3", "digit"}).head(6).to_md());
}
```
