> The method: cancellation replaces a complete scan

Let \(x=(x_0,\ldots,x_{c-1})\) be a configuration with \(q\) categories
per cell. Generate keys \(K_{i,v}\) for each cell \(i\) and category
\(v\ne0\), with \(K_{i,0}=0\). For a \(b\)-bit hash,

$$
H(x)=\bigoplus_{i=0}^{c-1}K_{i,x_i}.
$$
\alttext(H of x is the bitwise exclusive or over cells i of the key for cell i and its category x i.)

XOR is associative and commutative, and \(a\oplus a=0\). Replacing
\(x_j=u\) by \(v\) therefore gives

$$
H(x')=H(x)\oplus K_{j,u}\oplus K_{j,v}.
$$
\alttext(The updated hash equals the old hash exclusive or the old cell key exclusive or the replacement cell key.)

The old contribution cancels and the new one is inserted. Reversing the
replacement cancels the same two keys again, restoring the original hash.
For a set \(S\) of changed cells, the same argument gives

$$
H(y)=H(x)\oplus\bigoplus_{j\in S}\left(K_{j,x_j}\oplus K_{j,y_j}\right).
$$
\alttext(H of y equals H of x exclusive or, over changed cells, the old and new category keys.)

The result depends only on the final configuration, not the order of
updates. This is why positions reached by different move sequences can
share a transposition-table entry. Real game keys must also encode every
rule-relevant state variable, such as side to move and castling rights.
Here the configuration contains only pixels; the digit label is metadata.

Initialising the table costs \(O(cq)\) time and space; computing a full
hash costs \(O(c)\); one changed cell costs \(O(1)\). Updating \(k\)
known changes costs \(O(k)\), but discovering those changes by comparing
two complete images still costs \(O(c)\). This distinction prevents a
false speed claim for unrelated records.

> Applying it: turning the first digit into the second

`zobrist_boards` accepts the dataset directly after dropping its label.
It validates integer categories instead of silently rounding them.
`zobrist_transition` changes differing cells in index order and independently
recomputes each intermediate board's hash. Hashes are hexadecimal strings:
putting a 64-bit integer into a double would lose precision.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  auto pixels = dm::datasets::digits();
  pixels.remove_column("digit");
  const auto boards = dm::algorithms::zobrist_boards(pixels, 17);
  const auto table = dm::algorithms::zobrist_init(pixels.ncols(), 17, 2024);
  const auto trace = dm::algorithms::zobrist_transition(table, boards[0], boards[1]);
  std::print("{}\n", trace.head(8).to_md());
  std::print("\n| changed cells | verified updates |\n| ---: | ---: |\n| {} | {:.0f} |\n",
      trace.nrows(), dm::sum(trace.get_column("verified").values()));
}
```
