```{cpp, file="figures/zobrist_changes.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  auto pixels = dm::datasets::digits();
  pixels.remove_column("digit");
  const auto boards = dm::algorithms::zobrist_boards(pixels, 17);
  const auto t = dm::algorithms::zobrist_init(64, 17, 2024);
  const auto trace = dm::algorithms::zobrist_transition(t, boards[0], boards[1]);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .points(trace.get_column("cell").values(), trace.get_column("flipped_bits").values(),
              "changed hash bits", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 4)
      .abline_h(32, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 1)
      .title("One pixel replacement changes about half the hash bits")
      .x_label("changed pixel index").y_label("changed bits out of 64")
      .y_limits(0, 64).size(900, 460).save_svg("build/zobrist_changes.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/zobrist_changes.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Hash-bit changes during the transformation. The horizontal line is the ideal mean of 32, not an acceptance threshold.)
\alttext(A scatter plot of the number of changed hash bits against changed pixel index, with a reference line at 32 out of 64.)

For ideal independent keys a single nontrivial replacement produces a
uniform XOR difference, so the number of changed bits has distribution
\(\operatorname{Binomial}(64,1/2)\): mean 32 and standard deviation 4.
A large jump is therefore expected, but an attractive scatter plot alone
cannot establish randomness or security.

> Collisions: count pairs, and separate repeated data

Fix two distinct configurations \(x\ne y\). Choose any differing cell.
At least one nonzero-category key at that cell occurs exactly once in
\(H(x)\oplus H(y)\). Condition on all other keys: this remaining key is
uniform, so the XOR is zero with probability \(2^{-b}\). This proof
still works with category zero keyed to zero. The all-zero board itself
has a deterministic hash, so not every individual board hash is uniform.

For \(n\) distinct input boards, define a collision as an unordered pair
whose truncated hashes agree. Linearity of expectation gives

$$
C_b=\sum_{i<j}\mathbf{1}\{H_b(x_i)=H_b(x_j)\},\qquad
\mathbb{E}[C_b]=\binom{n}{2}2^{-b},\qquad
\Pr(C_b\ge1)\le\min\left(1,\binom{n}{2}2^{-b}\right).
$$
\alttext(C b counts matching pairs. Its expectation is n choose 2 times 2 to the minus b. The chance of any collision is at most the smaller of 1 and that expectation.)

The final inequality is the union bound; it needs no independence between
pair events. By contrast, \(1-\exp(-\mathbb{E}C_b)\) is a birthday/Poisson
approximation, not an exact theorem here. Repeated input boards must first
be removed: equal data are not hash failures. A bucket with \(r\) distinct
boards contributes \(r(r-1)/2\) pairs, not merely \(r-1\).

The library removes exact duplicates and averages over 64 consecutive
MT19937-64 seeds. Ideal-key theory is a model for those pseudorandom tables;
consecutive seeds are not a proof of independent randomness.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/zobrist_collisions.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  auto pixels = dm::datasets::digits();
  pixels.remove_column("digit");
  const auto boards = dm::algorithms::zobrist_boards(pixels, 17);
  std::print("| records | distinct pixel arrays |\n| ---: | ---: |\n| {} | {} |\n\n",
             pixels.nrows(), pixels.drop_duplicates().nrows());
  const auto profile = dm::algorithms::zobrist_collision_profile(boards, 17, {8, 10, 12, 14, 16, 20, 24, 32, 64});
  std::print("{}\n", profile.to_md());
  std::print("\n| retained bits | exact ideal expected pairs |\n| ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t r = 7; r < profile.nrows(); ++r)
    std::print("| {:.0f} | {:.8e} |\n", profile.double_at("bits", r), profile.double_at("expected_pairs", r));
  const auto visible = profile.head(7);
  auto observed = visible.get_column("mean_pairs").values();
  auto expected = visible.get_column("expected_pairs").values();
  for (auto& v : observed) v = std::log10(v);
  for (auto& v : expected) v = std::log10(v);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .line(visible.get_column("bits").values(), expected,
            "ideal expectation", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2)
      .points(visible.get_column("bits").values(), observed,
              "mean of 64 tables", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 5)
      .title("Colliding digit-image pairs after truncation")
      .x_label("retained low bits").y_label("log10(colliding pairs)")
      .size(900, 480).save_svg("build/zobrist_collisions.svg");
}
```
