\image(figures/zobrist_collisions.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Colliding pairs at different widths: mean, minimum and maximum over 64 tables, and ideal-key expectation. The figure shows the widths where collisions are measurable.)
\alttext(Mean observed collision counts and ideal expectations versus retained hash width. Counts decrease exponentially as more bits are retained.)

All 1797 images are distinct. At eight bits the observed mean is close to
the ideal expectation; at 24 bits only five pairs occur across all 64
tables. At 64 bits the ideal expected count per table is about
\(8.75\times10^{-14}\). Thus zero observed collisions at full width is
unsurprising and cannot validate such a small probability experimentally.
The additional scientific-notation rows preserve expectations that round
to zero in the main table.

> A deterministic dependency that survives every seed

Take four configurations that agree everywhere except at two cells, where
they contain respectively \((a,c),(a,d),(b,c),(b,d)\). Every key appears
an even number of times across their hashes. Consequently,

$$
H(a,c)\oplus H(a,d)\oplus H(b,c)\oplus H(b,d)=0.
$$
\alttext(The exclusive or of the four hashes at the corners of a two-cell category rectangle is always zero.)

Once three hashes are known the fourth is determined. Fully independent
uniform 64-bit hashes would satisfy this identity only with probability
\(2^{-64}\). This does not force a pair collision and does not contradict
the pairwise collision proof. It does rule out treating every collection
of configurations as fully independent, and makes Zobrist hashing unsuitable
as an adversarial integrity check.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto t = dm::algorithms::zobrist_init(2, 3, 2024);
  std::uint64_t rectangle = 0;
  std::print("| two-cell configuration | hash |\n| :--- | :--- |\n");
  for (const std::vector<int> x : {std::vector<int>{1, 1}, {1, 2}, {2, 1}, {2, 2}}) {
    const auto h = dm::algorithms::zobrist_hash(t, x);
    rectangle ^= h;
    std::print("| ({}, {}) | 0x{:016x} |\n", x[0], x[1], h);
  }
  std::print("| XOR of all four | 0x{:016x} |\n", rectangle);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| two-cell configuration | hash |
| :--- | :--- |
| (1, 1) | 0xd8daffcd1a59f103 |
| (1, 2) | 0xc94c64f7f77d1cac |
| (2, 1) | 0x8f709823cb5ab8b4 |
| (2, 2) | 0x9ee60319267e551b |
| XOR of all four | 0x0000000000000000 |
// result_end
\caption(A four-configuration XOR dependency, verified with the same public API used for the images.)

> Conclusion

The incremental identity holds at every changed pixel, and reduced-width
collisions can be measured against an exact expectation without claiming
full independence. At 64 bits and this dataset's size, the ideal union
bound is tiny; seeing no collisions provides little additional evidence.
A cache that must return exact answers should verify the stored configuration
after a hash match. Wider keys reduce accidental collisions but do not remove
the algebraic dependencies. For changing categorical states, the useful
property is cheap, reversible updates with a precisely defined collision model.

> References

- A. L. Zobrist (1970). *A New Hashing Method With Application for Game Playing*.
  University of Wisconsin, Technical Report 88.
  https://research.cs.wisc.edu/techreports/1970/TR88.pdf
- M. Pătraşcu and M. Thorup (2011 preprint). *The Power of Simple Tabulation Hashing*.
  https://arxiv.org/abs/1011.5200
